   351	/**
   352	 * **Phase 5.7 V3.4.0.4b (2026-05-23) -- UUID-derived 64-bit PeerId.**
   353	 *
   354	 * Generates a fresh `bigint` PeerId for {@link createSession} or
   355	 * {@link sessionFromQbook} (where the engine layer is peer-id-agnostic
   356	 * + the caller is responsible for ensuring uniqueness).
   357	 *
   358	 * **Source**: `crypto.randomUUID()` (Node 14.17+; Electron/VS Code well
   359	 * past that floor).  UUIDv4 has 122 bits of randomness across 16 bytes
   360	 * with 6 deterministic bits (4 bits for version `0100` at hex-char
   361	 * position 12 + 2 bits for variant `10` at hex-char position 16).
   362	 * Truncating to the first 16 hex chars (the first 64 bits) keeps ~60
   363	 * bits of entropy after extracting the version nibble at position 12
   364	 * (which is ALWAYS the literal hex `4`).  Birthday-paradox collision
   365	 * probability is therefore ~2^30 sessions before first collision --
   366	 * still effectively zero for real workbook usage (a user spawning ~1
   367	 * billion sessions in one workbook is not the threat model).  V3.4.0.X
   368	 * LOW-2 closure (cross-lane Codex L2 + Opus M3 doc-accuracy: the
   369	 * docstring previously claimed ~64 bits / ~2^32 collision).
   370	 *
   371	 * **Non-zero guarantee**: `PeerId(0)` is the engine's `LEGACY_PEER`
   372	 * sentinel + would assert-fail `CollabSession::new`.  Under spec-
   373	 * compliant UUIDv4, the first 16 hex chars CANNOT be all-zero (the
   374	 * version nibble at position 12 is the literal `4`, not `0`), so the
   375	 * all-zero outcome is IMPOSSIBLE.  The retry loop + 8-attempt cap is
   376	 * defense against a NON-SPEC `crypto.randomUUID` (entropy broken /
   377	 * unexpected impl); per CLAUDE.md No-Fallbacks, 8 consecutive non-spec
   378	 * UUIDs surface loudly via thrown error.
   379	 *
   380	 * **D5 deviation (V3.4.0.4b plan note)**: V3.4.0.1 D5 originally

