   260	    /// `LoroDoc` commit stream at construction (via `OpLog::new_undo_manager`).
   261	    /// It records every LOCAL commit made AFTER it is constructed — i.e. every
   262	    /// `oplog.append`, which `OpLog::append` follows with `doc.commit()`. It
   263	    /// holds an internal `Arc` to the doc, so it is `'static` and may be a
   264	    /// field (it does NOT borrow `self.oplog`).
   265	    ///
   266	    /// **Undo-unit granularity (HARD QUESTION 1).** `set_merge_interval(0)` is
   267	    /// set at construction → NO time-based merging, so each Loro commit is its
   268	    /// own undo unit. Every `WorkbookSession` mutating command produces EXACTLY
   269	    /// one commit: single-cell/structure/name/format/table edits each append
   270	    /// one op (one `append` = one `commit`), and `set_value`/`rename_sheet`/
   271	    /// `rename_table`/`rename_column` (the only commands whose underlying
   272	    /// producer can emit MULTIPLE ops) wrap them in a single `Op::BatchCommit`
   273	    /// (`cells.rs` `set_value` `match log_ops.len()`; `sheets.rs`/`tables.rs`
   274	    /// rename producers append one `BatchCommit`), and `batch`/`commit_transaction`
   275	    /// append one `Op::BatchCommit`. So one `undo()` reverts exactly one command
   276	    /// with NO explicit `group_start`/`group_end` needed. (Proven by
   277	    /// `undo_clear_of_formula_cell_is_one_unit` + `undo_batch_reverts_whole_batch`.)
   278	    ///
   279	    /// **Re-materialization model.** Unlike `ql_collab::CollabSession` (snapshot-
   280	    /// cache + on_push cell-meta partial invalidation), `WorkbookSession` owns a
   281	    /// LIVE workbook and re-materializes it WHOLESALE after a Loro undo/redo:
   282	    /// `replay_into` the (now-compacted) op-log into a fresh workbook, rebuild
   283	    /// the calcgraph, recompute, then `bump_epoch`. So no on_push meta encoding
   284	    /// is wired here — kept deliberately simpler. See [`rematerialize`].
   285	    ///
   286	    /// **v1 depth cap (audit L1).** Loro's `UndoManager::new` defaults to
   287	    /// `set_max_undo_steps(100)`; the session does not override it, so the undo
   288	    /// stack silently drops the oldest unit past 100 undoable commands. Bounded
   289	    /// retention is intentional for v1 (mirrors the `ops`/`events`/`change_log`
   290	    /// bounds); a configurable depth is a later increment.
   291	    ///
   292	    /// [`rematerialize`]: WorkbookSession::rematerialize
   293	    undo_manager: loro::UndoManager,
   294	    /// **6.4-3c (2026-05-29):** the out-of-process Python-UDF worker, if one is
   295	    /// configured (via [`set_udf_worker`]). `None` until the IDE/host injects
   296	    /// one. `RefCell` provides the interior mutability that bridges the shared-
   297	    /// `&` eval stack to [`ql_udf::UdfWorker::call`]'s `&mut self`: `with_runtime`
   298	    /// lends `&self.udf_worker` to the per-edit `WorkbookRuntime`, which threads
   299	    /// it into the eval env. Single-writer + single-threaded recompute, so the
   300	    /// `!Sync` `RefCell` is sound. A registered UDF with NO worker evaluates to
   301	    /// `#CALC!` at the dispatch site (No-Fallbacks-honest), never a panic. The
   302	    /// full napi/IDE injection bridge (+ `debugpy` / trusted-workspace gating) is
   303	    /// 6.4-3d; this field + [`set_udf_worker`] are the minimal in-engine surface.
   304	    ///
   305	    /// [`set_udf_worker`]: WorkbookSession::set_udf_worker
